is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: DNS service (BIND/named) on dns_server. The “local” authoritative zone lacks the hostname users are trying to reach.

Mechanism: Clients resolve names via 10.200.0.2 (confirmed in host_1_1_1_1 /etc/resolv.conf). The web stack itself is up (HTTP/1.1 200 OK from http://10.200.0.3 and http://web0.local), and network reachability is healthy. However, lookups for the main site name fail, so browsers can’t resolve the hostname and report the page as down even though the server responds by IP.

Localization: On dns_server, named listens on port 53 (netstat shows 10.200.0.2:53). BIND is authoritative for zone “local” (named.conf, named.conf.local) with zone data in /etc/bind/db.local. That zone contains A records for web0–web3 but no intranet or www records. From host_1_1_1_1, dig +short @10.200.0.2 web0.local resolves, but dig +short @10.200.0.2 intranet.local returns no data, and curl http://intranet.local fails.

Root cause: The intranet site’s DNS name (e.g., intranet.local, possibly www.local) is missing from the “local” zone file on dns_server.

Corrective fix: Add a record in /etc/bind/db.local, e.g., “intranet IN CNAME web0.local.” (or “intranet IN A 10.200.0.3”), and optionally “www IN CNAME web0.local.” Increment the SOA serial, then reload BIND (e.g., rndc reload or systemctl reload bind9). After reload, verify with “dig @10.200.0.2 intranet.local” and confirm the page loads by name from the client. Optionally set a search domain on clients if users type bare “intranet”. 
=== END ===